# Expectation record for 03_initializers/004_designated_array.c
#
# Designated array initializers: sparse and out-of-order designators, the C11
# current-object repositioning rule, implied array size, constant-expression
# indices, two-dimensional designators, and array-of-struct designators.
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_designated_array
area               = 03_initializers
description        = Designated array initializers -- sparse and out-of-order designators, designated-then-positional continuation exercising the C11 current-object rule, array size implied by the largest designator, constant-expression indices, two-dimensional row and element designators, and array-of-struct designators -- for static and automatic storage duration, every element read back individually
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined and no unspecified behaviour. That is the precondition which
makes both differential oracles sound: undefined behaviour would license either compiler to do
anything at all, so a divergence over it would prove nothing about either one.

The one rule this program has to satisfy that an ordinary initializer program does not is C11
6.7.9p23: the evaluations of an initializer list's expressions are indeterminately sequenced with
respect to one another, so the order in which their SIDE EFFECTS occur is unspecified. Every
initializer expression here is an integer constant expression -- 1, 10 through 13, 30, 50, 70, 101,
110 through 113, 130, 170, and the small constants in the two-dimensional and struct cases -- and a
constant expression has no side effects at all, so that unspecified order is unobservable. Which
SLOT each initializer lands in is a separate rule, fixed by C11 6.7.9p19, which initializes in
initializer-list order; that is what several of these forms depend on for their answer, and it is
not what 6.7.9p23 leaves open.

Every object is fully initialized before any read. Elements not named by a designator are implicitly
initialized to zero under C11 6.7.9p19 and p21, so every gap between sparse designators holds a
definite zero rather than an indeterminate value. There is no read of uninitialized storage. That
applies to the two automatic-duration objects exactly as it does to the nine static ones, because an
object with any initializer at all is fully initialized.

EVERY DESIGNATOR INDEX IS IN RANGE. g_sparse and l_sparse are declared [8] and use
indices 0, 3 and 7. g_continue, l_continue and g_constexpr are declared [6] and use
indices 0, 1, 2 and 3, with the positional initializers following [1] landing at
[2] and [3]. g_implied is declared with an unspecified bound, and C11 6.7.9p22
makes its size the largest designated index plus one, so its length is exactly 5
and index 4 is its last element. The two-dimensional objects are declared [2][3]
and use row indices 0 and 1 and element indices 0, 1 and 2. g_st_memb and
g_st_brace are declared [3] and use indices 0 and 2.

The two constant-expression indices [2 + 1] and [1 * 2] are integer constant
expressions evaluated at translation time to 3 and 2, both within the declared
bound of 6. No signed overflow is possible in either.

No arithmetic is performed on any initialized value. The only run-time arithmetic
is on the loop counters i and j, each bounded strictly below its extent. Every
subscript is in bounds; no one-past-end pointer is formed and none is
dereferenced.

No shifts, no pointer casts, no aliasing -- each object is read only through its
own declared type. No object is modified after initialization, so nothing is
modified twice between sequence points. Each printf call has at most one argument
with a side effect, and none has one. No padding byte is read: only named members
are accessed, no struct is printed as a whole, and no object representation is
inspected.

g_implied_len is computed as (int)(sizeof g_implied / sizeof g_implied[0]). The two
sizeof operands cancel, so this is an ELEMENT COUNT rather than a byte size and is
5 on every target irrespective of pointer or long width. The read loop over g_implied uses that
same computed count rather than a literal 5, so an array the compiler sized wrongly shows up as a
wrong g_implied_len line rather than as a read past the end of the object.

Every conversion specifier is %d with an argument of type int after the default argument promotions,
so no variadic argument is read at the wrong width. main returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite
requires so that no exit status is truncated by the operating system. Nothing outside the source file
is consulted: every input is a literal here, no file, socket, environment variable, clock, random
source or locale is read, and iteration order is fixed, so every cell is reproducible from this
program and this record alone.

No header is included anywhere and printf is declared by hand as `int printf(const char *, ...);`.
bcc bundles the nine required freestanding headers plus a bonus stdatomic.h and ships no standard
I/O header (docs/project-guide.md line 212), so naming one would fail on the bcc side of oracle (a)
while succeeding on the reference side -- a spurious divergence caused by the test rather than by a
compiler. This area is granted no header exception; the corpus has exactly two, area 07's stdarg.h
and the dedicated bundled-header probe in area 12.

This record declares no warning-gate deviation, and area 03 sanctions none. The program is clean
under the full default gate -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror
at -O0, -O1 and -O2 with all four reference drivers, with no diagnostic of any kind and no
source-level diagnostic suppression anywhere in the translation unit, and it builds and runs clean
under the sanitizer gate -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all. Both gates are
reference-compiler-only and are never applied to bcc, so requirement 3's shared-flag discipline is
untouched.

REASONING ON OVERLAPPING DESIGNATORS, AS THE AREA REQUIREMENTS DIRECT:

The area requirements permit overlapping designators "only where the later
initializer's precedence is well defined". Two distinct situations must be
separated here.

First, the situation this program DOES exercise. In g_continue the initializer
{ [1] = 11, 12, 13, [0] = 10 } contains no two initializers for the same element.
The designator [1] repositions the current object; the following positional
initializers 12 and 13 therefore initialize [2] and [3] under C11 6.7.9p17 and
p18; the trailing designator [0] then repositions again and initializes [0]
without disturbing anything already set. Elements [4] and [5] are implicitly zero.
The resulting order 10, 11, 12, 13, 0, 0 is fully determined by the standard, so
this is a well-defined use of interleaved designated and positional initializers
and it is included. The same reasoning applies to the two-dimensional objects
g_row_then_elem and g_elem_then_row, whose designators address disjoint elements
and which therefore produce identical results from opposite initializer orders.
Four further forms turn on the same rule without naming a slot twice: g_elem_only supplies three
element designators out of row order, g_st_brace lists its element braces [2] before [0], g_st_memb
interleaves member designators across two elements, and g_sparse and l_sparse designate descending
indices 7, 3, 0. Each is gate-clean precisely because no initializer in it revisits a slot an
earlier one already named.

Second, the situation this program DELIBERATELY EXCLUDES, with its reason, per
constraint C3's requirement to "state so explicitly and explain why". A duplicate
designator that supplies a SECOND initializer for an element already initialized,
for example { [0] = 1, [1] = 2, [1] = 20, [2] = 3 }, is well defined in C11 -- the
later initializer takes precedence -- but it cannot be compiled under this suite's
mandated authoring gate. The gate includes -Wextra, which enables
-Woverride-init, and -Werror, which promotes it to a hard error. Fourteen
spellings of a value-overriding designator were probed with the reference
compiler and every one is rejected with "initialized field overwritten
[-Werror=override-init]": a duplicate element designator; an override carrying the SAME value; a
zero-then-value override; a whole-row brace then one element of that row; that pair reversed, an
element then the whole-row brace; the reversed pair again with a later initializer for a different
row; positional continuation running back over an earlier designator; a struct-element brace then
one member of it; two member designators then a whole-struct brace over them; a duplicate struct
member designator; two union member designators; a nested whole-member brace then one member of it;
the reverse of that nesting; and an override inside a compound literal.
There is therefore no gate-clean formulation of this spelling, and area 03
sanctions no deviation from the default gate, so no ub_audit_flags override is
used. An override carrying a side effect is not an escape from the diagnostic either:
-Woverride-init-side-effects is enabled by DEFAULT and is not a gate member at all, and the
sequencing that C11 6.7.9p23 leaves unspecified would then become observable, which requirement 1
forbids outright. Nor is a source-level diagnostic-suppression directive an alternative: it
neutralizes a gate member from inside the translation unit while this record still claims the
unchanged gate, which would make this record's own claim false rather than making the program clean.

A range designator such as { [0 ... 3] = 7 } would express a related idea, but it
is a GCC extension rather than C11, it is rejected by -pedantic which the gate also
enables -- measured, as "ISO C forbids specifying range of elements to initialize
[-Werror=pedantic]" -- and GCC extensions are the subject of area 08 rather than area 03.

The exclusion is limited to that one SPELLING. The FEATURE under test -- designated
array initializers -- remains fully covered by the six gate-clean forms this
program exercises: sparse and out-of-order designators, designated-then-positional
continuation, array size implied by the largest designator,
constant-expression indices, two-dimensional row-then-element and
element-then-row and element-only designators, and array-of-struct designators in
both member and brace form. Nothing is dropped for difficulty. What the exclusion does cost is
stated rather than left to be inferred: no program in this area distinguishes "the last initializer
to name a subobject wins" from "any explicitly named subobject survives", and that remains a gap
which no program in the corpus closes.

This is a diagnostic policy of the reference compiler's authoring gate, not a
limitation of the compiler under test, so it is recorded here rather than as an
expected-divergence marker.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This record narrows nothing that the harness reads: all four targets, all three optimization levels
and all three oracles are enabled, no expected-divergence marker is carried, no gate deviation is
claimed under the ub_audit_flags key, and no implementation-defined value reaches this program's
output. One initializer SPELLING is nevertheless excluded from the source, and because that
exclusion is an undefined-and-unspecified-behaviour matter rather than an implementation-defined
one, it is stated where the area requirements direct: in ub_notes above, under REASONING ON
OVERLAPPING DESIGNATORS.

All printed values are int and are printed with %d only. The program contains no
long, size_t, ptrdiff_t, intptr_t, float, double or long double. The single sizeof
use is the cancelling element-count division for g_implied_len, which yields 5 on
every target, so the measured width difference between i686 (4-byte long and
pointer) and the other three targets (8-byte) cannot affect the output. That difference is
documented as well as measured: i686 is ELF32 with a four-byte long and pointer while x86_64,
aarch64 and riscv64 are ELF64 with eight bytes for both (docs/technical-specifications.md target
table, lines 457-462). No sizeof result is printed as a byte count and no _Alignof result is printed
at all.

No character data is used, so the measured plain-char signedness difference
(signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) cannot arise. Nothing
character-valued is printed either, so C11 5.2.1 leaves nothing here to the execution character
set.

No address or pointer value is printed, and no struct is printed as a whole, so
padding bytes -- whose contents are unspecified -- are never observed. These all-int aggregates
carry no internal padding on any of the four targets in any case, and because no address is printed
no layout choice the ABI is free to make is observable: only the values the initializers supply are.

g_row_then_elem and g_elem_then_row are constructed to produce IDENTICAL output
from OPPOSITE initializer orders. Any asymmetry between those two line groups is
an unambiguous defect signal rather than an implementation-defined difference.

The discriminating variant here is the pairing of storage durations, which is also why all three
optimization levels matter: a static designated initializer is resolved before program startup and
emitted into the data image, whereas its automatic twin's placement is a run-time act the backend
performs on entry to the block, and at a higher level either path may be folded into immediate
operands. Every element of every object is read back individually, so a value landing in the wrong
slot, a disturbed neighbour or a lost zero fill is each its own line, and nothing is summarized.

Output is byte-identical on all four targets at all three optimization levels,
verified by measurement. The program was compiled with the reference driver for each target at -O0,
-O1 and -O2 -- twelve configurations -- and every one produced these 70 lines byte-identically with
exit status 0, which is why oracle (b) compares this program's targets against each other with no
narrowing and why oracle (c) can hold these bytes as the golden record.
END
